<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fernando Belaúnde Terry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fernando Belaúnde Terry was a Peruvian architect and politician, founder of the Popular Action party, who was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from 1963 to 1968 and from 1980 to 1985. His first government, which promoted roads and settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, was overthrown by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-velasco-alvarado">Juan Velasco Alvarado</a>. Returning in 1980 as the military handed back power, he faced the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> insurgency, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> of 1983 and economic crisis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fernando-belaunde-terry.jpg" alt="Fernando Belaúnde Terry" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 144155804@N02 (Flickr) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fernando_Bela%C3%BAnde_Terry.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fernando Belaúnde was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 7 October 1912, the second of four children of a wealthy family. His father, Rafael Belaúnde Diez Canseco, was a professor who later served as prime minister under José Bustamante y Rivero. Persecution of the family during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-b-leguia">Augusto B. Leguía</a> led them to move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1924, where he began university studies in engineering. From 1930 to 1935 he studied architecture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, at the University of Miami and then the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> at Austin. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1936 he designed private homes, founded the magazine El Arquitecto Peruano in 1937 and from 1943 taught architecture and urban planning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1944 he co-founded the National Democratic Front, which elected Bustamante president in 1945, and sat in Congress until General Manuel Odría's coup of 1948. In 1956 he led a slate for election. When the electoral board refused his candidacy, he led a protest that police met with water <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, known as the manguerazo, and crossed alone to the police chief carrying a Peruvian flag; the government gave way. He lost to Manuel Prado and in July 1956 founded the Acción Popular party at Chincheros, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>. In 1959 he was jailed for twelve days at El Frontón. In 1962 he came second, by under 14,000 votes, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-raul-haya-de-la-torre">Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre</a>; the military deposed Prado, and Belaúnde won the new election of 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Belaúnde's first term promoted a doctrine he called the Conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> by Peruvians, aimed at developing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> and other outlying regions. It included the Carretera Marginal de la Selva, a highway linking Chiclayo with Amazonas and San Martín, irrigation and hydroelectric projects, the Banco de la Nación and social housing. In 1964 the armed forces dropped napalm on the Matsés after two loggers were killed, killing hundreds. By 1967 the sol had lost much of its value. A settlement with a Standard Oil subsidiary over the La Brea and Pariñas oil fields forced his cabinet to resign on 1 October 1968, and days later General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-velasco-alvarado">Juan Velasco Alvarado</a> overthrew him. He spent the next decade teaching in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>Elected again in April 1980 with 45 percent of the vote, he returned newspapers to their owners and finished the highway. The internal conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> began the day before the election; states of emergency were declared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> and Apurímac, military officers reportedly committed human rights violations, and thousands died. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> of 1982–83 brought floods and droughts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-garcia">Alan García</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a> won in 1985, and Belaúnde became senator for life. He died on 4 June 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fernando Belaúnde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1970" aria-label="Lima on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2433 2613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Fernando Belaúnde Terry"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2883 2883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2883 2883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-12.06,-77.04&amp;year=1970">What was here in 1970? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
